# HTTP/1.1 message golden manifest (RFC 7230).
#
# Each row names a byte-exact `.http` corpus file (sibling of this
# manifest, CRLF line endings preserved on disk) and the salient
# parse expectations NET-7.3's incremental parser must produce. The
# raw bytes are the source of truth for the wire; this manifest pins
# the decoded head + body-framing decision.
#
# Format (fields '|'-separated):
#   <file> | <kind> | <start-line-field> | <framing> | <body-len> | <keep-alive>
#
#   kind              -> request | response
#   start-line-field  -> for request: "<METHOD> <target>"
#                        for response: "<status> <reason>"
#   framing           -> content-length | chunked | close | none
#   body-len          -> decoded body length in bytes (-1 if streamed/unknown,
#                        e.g. chunked or close-delimited)
#   keep-alive        -> 1 if the connection is reusable per HTTP/1.1
#                        defaults (1.1 keep-alive unless Connection: close), else 0
#
request-get-simple.http        | request  | GET /hello.txt        | none           | 0   | 1
request-post-contentlength.http| request  | POST /submit          | content-length | 17  | 1
response-200-contentlength.http| response | 200 OK                | content-length | 13  | 1
response-chunked.http          | response | 200 OK                | chunked        | -1  | 1
response-301-redirect.http     | response | 301 Moved Permanently | content-length | 0   | 1
response-204-close.http        | response | 204 No Content        | close          | -1  | 0
